Short flags: -l
Long flags: --list
File Permissions (ls -l output)

1) Common Permission: -rw-r--r--
Meaning: Everyone can read the file, but only the owner can edit it.
Breakdown:
- → Regular file
rw- → Owner: Read + Write
r-- → Group: Read only
r-- → Others: Read only

2) Strict Permission: -rw-------
Meaning: Only the owner can read or write this file. Very secure/private.
Breakdown:
- → Regular file
rw- → Owner: Read + Write
--- → Group: No access
--- → Others: No access
Numeric value: 600
Used for: SSH private keys, passwords, API keys, secret config files.

3) Directory Example: drwx------
Meaning: Only the owner can access this folder.
Breakdown:
d → Directory
rwx → Owner: Full access
--- → Group: No access
--- → Others: No access

Common Numeric Permissions:
777 → Everyone can do everything
755 → Owner full, others read & execute (common for folders)
644 → Owner can edit, others read only (common for files)
600 → Only owner can read & write
700 → Only owner can access (for folders)

Important Directories
/ → Root (start of the filesystem)
/home → User home directories
/etc → Configuration files
/var → Logs and variable data
/tmp → Temporary files

Navigation
cd <folder> → Go to folder
cd .. → Go up one level
cd \~ → Go to home directory
cd - → Go back to previous directory
pwd → Show current directory

User & Privilege Commands
su → Switch User
Used to switch to another user account.
su - or su -l → Switch user with login environment
This is the proper way when you want to fully log in as the other user (loads their full environment, home directory, shell settings, etc.).
Example: su - root or su -l username
sudo → Superuser do (run a single command as root)
sudo -i or sudo su → Get a full root login shell

Why Permissions Matter
Linux is a multi-user system. Permissions help:
Control who can read, modify, or execute files
Prevent accidents and unauthorized access
Keep the system secure


